Pouch link and linkat answer EPERM (operator vote 2026-10-21).

Thylacine makes no hard links. link() and linkat() reached the 0xFFFF
sentinel (0001) and answered ENOSYS, which ported code does not expect from
them: fontconfig's cache lock falls back to a directory lock only on EPERM,
ENOTSUP or EACCES, so it could never write a cache. Both now answer EPERM,
Linux's errno for a filesystem that cannot make hard links
(dec-2026-10-21-pouch-link-eperm, docs/ERRORS.md "Hard links").

linkat refuses a flag outside AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW | AT_EMPTY_PATH with EINVAL
first, as Linux does. Neither call reads a path or a descriptor, and nothing
traps, so a missing source, an existing target, a bad descriptor, a read-only
filesystem and a link across two filesystems all read EPERM, where Linux
resolves the paths first and answers ENOENT, EEXIST, EBADF, EROFS or EXDEV.
A caller's fallback (a rename, a copy, an O_EXCL create, a mkdir) meets those
errors itself. Answering before the paths also keeps one lock protocol:
fontconfig takes its directory lock whether or not one is already held, so it
recovers a stale lock with rmdir. Were EEXIST answered first, it would unlink
the stale directory, fail, and never recover it (fcatomic.c FcAtomicLock).

musl's one other caller, sem_open, never reaches link here: its MAP_SHARED
map is refused first (0047).

--- a/src/unistd/link.c
+++ b/src/unistd/link.c
@@ -1,12 +1,11 @@
+/* pouch boundary-line: Thylacine makes no hard links (operator vote 2026-10-21,
+ * docs/ERRORS.md "Hard links"). EPERM is Linux's answer for a filesystem that
+ * cannot make one; neither path is read and nothing traps. */
 #include <unistd.h>
-#include <fcntl.h>
+#include <errno.h>
 #include "syscall.h"
 
 int link(const char *existing, const char *new)
 {
-#ifdef SYS_link
-	return syscall(SYS_link, existing, new);
-#else
-	return syscall(SYS_linkat, AT_FDCWD, existing, AT_FDCWD, new, 0);
-#endif
+	return __syscall_ret(-EPERM);
 }
--- a/src/unistd/linkat.c
+++ b/src/unistd/linkat.c
@@ -1,7 +1,15 @@
+/* pouch boundary-line: no hard links, as link.c. A flag outside
+ * AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW | AT_EMPTY_PATH is EINVAL first, as on Linux; neither
+ * descriptor nor path is read. */
+#define _BSD_SOURCE
 #include <unistd.h>
+#include <fcntl.h>
+#include <errno.h>
 #include "syscall.h"
 
 int linkat(int fd1, const char *existing, int fd2, const char *new, int flag)
 {
-	return syscall(SYS_linkat, fd1, existing, fd2, new, flag);
+	if (flag & ~(AT_SYMLINK_FOLLOW | AT_EMPTY_PATH))
+		return __syscall_ret(-EINVAL);
+	return __syscall_ret(-EPERM);
 }
